From: Codex <codex@openai.com>
Subject: [PATCH] radio: service protocol timers under frame load

Each em_t radio owns a protocol thread and queue separate from the manager
queue fixed by 0053.  proto_run() called proto_timeout() only when its timed
condition wait expired.  A continuously non-empty frame/command queue therefore
starved the radio state machine indefinitely.

This defeats the bounded WSC-M1 retransmission in 0021.  During the first cold
reconstruction of a clean rev120 VM, all three bpiap-001 radios sent M1 at
03:53:29.  The controller answered only the 5 and 6 GHz radios.  The 2.4 GHz
radio remained in wsc_m2_pending for more than four minutes without emitting
any of the expected M1 retries while its queue continued to receive normal
protocol traffic.  The runtime correctly failed with only two configured
fronthaul bands and zero process restarts.

Track the radio's timeout deadline with a monotonic clock and service one due
protocol tick between queued events as well as after an idle timed wait.  Do
not replay missed ticks in a burst: state-machine retry counters represent
elapsed opportunities, and one transition per event boundary is sufficient to
restore progress without racing the radio thread.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 src/em/em.cpp | 22 +++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 21 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/em/em.cpp b/src/em/em.cpp
--- a/src/em/em.cpp
+++ b/src/em/em.cpp
@@ -38,6 +38,7 @@
 #include <sys/un.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
 #include <pthread.h>
+#include <chrono>
 #include <openssl/rand.h>
 #include <openssl/evp.h>
 #include <openssl/dh.h>
@@ -620,6 +621,24 @@ void em_t::proto_run()
     struct timespec time_to_wait;
     struct timeval tm;
 
+    using steady_clock = std::chrono::steady_clock;
+    const auto timeout_interval = std::chrono::seconds(m_iq.timeout);
+    auto next_timeout = steady_clock::now() + timeout_interval;
+
+    // A busy radio queue must not suppress protocol timers.  In particular,
+    // wsc_m2_pending uses these ticks to retransmit M1 when one of several
+    // simultaneous radio M2 responses is lost.  This runs on the existing
+    // radio thread, so state transitions remain serialized with frames.
+    auto run_due_timeout = [&]() {
+        const auto now = steady_clock::now();
+        if (now < next_timeout) {
+            return;
+        }
+        proto_timeout();
+        // Resume from now rather than replaying delayed state transitions.
+        next_timeout = now + timeout_interval;
+    };
+
     pthread_mutex_lock(&m_iq.lock);
     while (m_exit == false) {
         rc = 0;
@@ -650,11 +668,12 @@ void em_t::proto_run()
                     proto_process(&cevnt); // pass pointer
                 }
                 free(evt);
+                run_due_timeout();
                 pthread_mutex_lock(&m_iq.lock);
             }
         } else if (rc == ETIMEDOUT) {
             pthread_mutex_unlock(&m_iq.lock);
-            proto_timeout();
+            run_due_timeout();
             pthread_mutex_lock(&m_iq.lock);
         } else {
             printf("%s:%d em exited with rc - %d\n",__func__,__LINE__,rc);

